WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.675 --> 00:00:02.640
- Once I started to generously serve,

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.306
my product got better as an entrepreneur.

00:00:04.306 --> 00:00:07.223
How many of you have a people plan?

00:00:08.848 --> 00:00:10.970
See, that's the greatest opportunity

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:11.968
and why my company exists.

00:00:11.968 --> 00:00:13.471
Because the last time I met you

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:15.949
I don't think I was my authentic self.

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:17.508
I don't think I was Keith Ferrazzi.

00:00:17.508 --> 00:00:21.022
To me, mentorship is a
very misunderstood idea.

00:00:21.022 --> 00:00:23.990
Leadership is about earning
permission to influence

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.838
and the role doesn't give
you permission to influence.

00:00:26.838 --> 00:00:28.668
How many times have you backed down

00:00:28.668 --> 00:00:31.268
from what you knew you needed to do

00:00:31.268 --> 00:00:33.503
because you were afraid to ask.

00:00:33.503 --> 00:00:35.653
Most intimate critical relationships

00:00:35.653 --> 00:00:37.589
lie to each other.

00:00:37.589 --> 00:00:38.727
And they shouldn't.

00:00:38.727 --> 00:00:40.185
Small doses of incremental positive

00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:42.302
experiences change mindsets.

00:00:42.302 --> 00:00:44.167
So you change the behavior
to change the mindset.

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:46.511
The key is we recognize
that inside of all of us,

00:00:46.511 --> 00:00:47.666
I think is greatness.

00:00:47.666 --> 00:00:49.535
I think extraordinary things and we need

00:00:49.535 --> 00:00:50.773
other people to pull them out.

00:00:50.773 --> 00:00:52.239
If you take nothing away from the time

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:54.191
that we're going to
together going forward,

00:00:54.191 --> 00:00:56.015
if you do nothing else,
but just remember this.

00:00:56.015 --> 00:00:59.027
- He's an American
author and found and CEO

00:00:59.027 --> 00:01:00.705
of Ferrazzi Greenlight.

00:01:00.705 --> 00:01:02.498
His first book Never Eat Alone

00:01:02.498 --> 00:01:05.488
was published in 2005
and became a bestseller.

00:01:05.488 --> 00:01:07.885
He's been described as a master networker.

00:01:07.885 --> 00:01:10.094
He's Keith Ferrazzi and here's my take

00:01:10.094 --> 00:01:12.245
on his top ten rules for success.

00:01:12.245 --> 00:01:14.578
(swooshing)

00:01:15.728 --> 00:01:17.125
- You know, in retrospect in my life

00:01:17.125 --> 00:01:19.361
I spent way too much time early on

00:01:19.361 --> 00:01:22.026
as an entrepreneur pushing selling

00:01:22.026 --> 00:01:25.521
as opposed to generously serving

00:01:25.521 --> 00:01:28.825
and once I started to generously serve,

00:01:28.825 --> 00:01:31.999
my product got better as an entrepreneur.

00:01:31.999 --> 00:01:34.763
So, even in that area of
professional currency,

00:01:34.763 --> 00:01:36.835
people who believe that their job

00:01:36.835 --> 00:01:39.377
is to sell stuff versus serve a community

00:01:39.377 --> 00:01:41.711
actually don't do as well.

00:01:41.711 --> 00:01:45.043
Now how many of you in this
room have a to do list?

00:01:45.043 --> 00:01:46.605
Raise your hands.

00:01:46.605 --> 00:01:49.188
Of course, you do, because productivity

00:01:49.188 --> 00:01:50.200
is important, isn't it?

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.608
How many of you have a people plan?

00:01:51.608 --> 00:01:54.348
See that's the greatest opportunity

00:01:54.348 --> 00:01:55.726
and why my company exists.

00:01:55.726 --> 00:01:57.476
Because if your relationships

00:01:57.476 --> 00:01:59.898
are so crucially important to your success

00:01:59.898 --> 00:02:03.328
in so many ways, why is
it that you have not asked

00:02:03.328 --> 00:02:06.115
yourself a simple question every year?

00:02:06.115 --> 00:02:07.798
Who are the 25 most important people

00:02:07.798 --> 00:02:10.054
to my success and how do I deepen

00:02:10.054 --> 00:02:13.632
those relationships
authentically and genuinely?

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:15.147
- It's an extraordinary
book, Who's Got Your Back.

00:02:15.147 --> 00:02:17.086
You write about the
courage to be vulnerable.

00:02:17.086 --> 00:02:19.971
I guess that's true, give me an example.

00:02:19.971 --> 00:02:23.971
- I'll give you one
right now and I'll do it.

00:02:23.971 --> 00:02:27.201
I'm sitting here sweating a little bit

00:02:27.201 --> 00:02:29.801
because you don't see it, I feel it

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:31.999
because the last time I met you,

00:02:31.999 --> 00:02:34.897
I don't think I was my authentic self.

00:02:34.897 --> 00:02:37.054
I don't think I was Keith Ferrazzi.

00:02:37.054 --> 00:02:38.762
I think I was some guy trying to impress.

00:02:38.762 --> 00:02:40.916
I met you at a dinner, you
may not even remember it.

00:02:40.916 --> 00:02:41.914
- Chicago.

00:02:41.914 --> 00:02:42.927
- In Chicago.

00:02:42.927 --> 00:02:43.923
We were being interviewed on stage

00:02:43.923 --> 00:02:46.666
and I was so excited to meet you.

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:48.875
You know, obviously, the
platform that you have,

00:02:48.875 --> 00:02:51.440
the potential that if we actually clicked

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.531
and, you know, I believe in this stuff

00:02:53.531 --> 00:02:56.092
and I want to create a movement of people

00:02:56.092 --> 00:02:58.493
connecting with other
people more authentically

00:02:58.493 --> 00:03:01.175
and seriously and when I
get nervous, I get bigger

00:03:01.175 --> 00:03:04.008
and sometimes I brag and I perform

00:03:05.622 --> 00:03:08.668
and other people get quiet and turn in,

00:03:08.668 --> 00:03:09.668
but I go out.

00:03:09.668 --> 00:03:11.311
And I did that at dinner
and I just felt myself

00:03:11.311 --> 00:03:13.668
going further and further away from you,

00:03:13.668 --> 00:03:16.593
which somebody I admired
and wanted to get closer to.

00:03:16.593 --> 00:03:20.128
To me, mentorship is a
very misunderstood idea.

00:03:20.128 --> 00:03:23.211
Most people think of mentors of let's

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.122
find a guy like Louis
here who we look up to

00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:28.702
and would like to aspire to be and please

00:03:28.702 --> 00:03:32.342
allow me to learn everything at your feet.

00:03:32.342 --> 00:03:33.921
That's kind of (bleep).

00:03:33.921 --> 00:03:35.024
- Which doesn't work.

00:03:35.024 --> 00:03:36.160
- Well, I don't know if it works or not.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:37.584
The bottom line is you are a mentor.

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:39.186
The lunch that we had together,

00:03:39.186 --> 00:03:41.769
you coming up to my house, what I learned

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:46.379
from your podcast from your
work teaches me something.

00:03:46.379 --> 00:03:50.587
And I can't find, I need
to find a group of people

00:03:50.587 --> 00:03:52.822
in my life constantly
that I'm learning from.

00:03:52.822 --> 00:03:54.077
They're my mentors.

00:03:54.077 --> 00:03:55.074
- Yeah.

00:03:55.074 --> 00:03:56.944
- I don't try to rest too much weight

00:03:56.944 --> 00:03:59.121
in any one individual.

00:03:59.121 --> 00:04:01.861
Our job is to create a set a road map

00:04:01.861 --> 00:04:06.326
of individuals in our lives
through whom we learn.

00:04:06.326 --> 00:04:08.240
Right, and I just started,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.255
I was chatting with you a second ago.

00:04:10.255 --> 00:04:12.448
I started a new high tech mobile

00:04:12.448 --> 00:04:15.171
enterprise software company.

00:04:15.171 --> 00:04:16.877
I am so out of my element.

00:04:16.877 --> 00:04:18.910
But, you know, I'm raising money.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.891
I'm hiring individuals, I'm
coming up with strategy,

00:04:21.891 --> 00:04:23.966
I'm building a sales
force, I'm doing a lot

00:04:23.966 --> 00:04:25.791
of the stuff I know how
to do, a lot of stuff

00:04:25.791 --> 00:04:26.793
I don't know how to do.

00:04:26.793 --> 00:04:29.309
I am finding mentors left and right.

00:04:29.309 --> 00:04:30.734
It's survival.

00:04:30.734 --> 00:04:31.727
- You are, yeah.

00:04:31.727 --> 00:04:34.228
- So mentorship is about
relational learning

00:04:34.228 --> 00:04:36.644
and if you think about it that way,

00:04:36.644 --> 00:04:38.171
then you don't have a mentor,

00:04:38.171 --> 00:04:40.004
you have many mentors.

00:04:41.335 --> 00:04:42.333
- Around your learning roadmap.

00:04:42.333 --> 00:04:44.957
- Leadership is about earning
permission to influence

00:04:44.957 --> 00:04:47.594
and the role doesn't give
you permission to influence.

00:04:47.594 --> 00:04:50.294
Most people think that the role does.

00:04:50.294 --> 00:04:54.461
It's so, you know (laughs)
I've had conversations

00:04:55.943 --> 00:04:57.920
with names that you
would know who are CEOs

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.683
of large corporations, we laugh together

00:05:00.683 --> 00:05:02.917
on how the world thinks
that all they have to do

00:05:02.917 --> 00:05:06.917
is say what they want
and people will comply.

00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:07.916
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:05:07.916 --> 00:05:10.168
- Nobody complies.

00:05:10.168 --> 00:05:12.277
Nobody complies.

00:05:12.277 --> 00:05:13.703
People will do what they want to do.

00:05:13.703 --> 00:05:15.675
They might do enough not to get fired

00:05:15.675 --> 00:05:19.189
or not to be, you know,
openly disrespectful,

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:22.275
but deep down inside, they
do what they want to do.

00:05:22.275 --> 00:05:23.276
- [Interviewer] Sure.

00:05:23.276 --> 00:05:26.079
- And leadership has
to work their ass off,

00:05:26.079 --> 00:05:30.323
vulnerably, intimately,
empathetically, generously.

00:05:30.323 --> 00:05:31.701
You used the word generosity.

00:05:31.701 --> 00:05:35.849
Generously to earn the right
to ask someone to change

00:05:35.849 --> 00:05:37.202
and that's a full-time job.

00:05:37.202 --> 00:05:40.017
If any of you lead people,

00:05:40.017 --> 00:05:43.430
if you're not spending 30% of your time,

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.597
30% of your time making
each of your employees

00:05:49.665 --> 00:05:53.832
30% better, right, you're
not doing your job as leader.

00:05:56.312 --> 00:05:58.262
As opposed to most of your time being

00:05:58.262 --> 00:06:00.370
in meetings and dictating (bleep).

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.140
That's a big shift.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:03.562
I'm not just going to tell my employee

00:06:03.562 --> 00:06:04.804
to do something today.

00:06:04.804 --> 00:06:08.254
I've got to make them 30% better, crap.

00:06:08.254 --> 00:06:10.249
That's a big investment.

00:06:10.249 --> 00:06:11.672
A big investment.

00:06:11.672 --> 00:06:13.513
Now I come in and say,
okay, now how do I do that.

00:06:13.513 --> 00:06:14.895
Well, the first thing you got to do

00:06:14.895 --> 00:06:16.060
is you got to learn permission to.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:19.270
If I gain vulnerability,
empathy, sympathy, care,

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:22.661
now I'm working with gravity, right?

00:06:22.661 --> 00:06:26.277
And so you got to open
up gravity for yourself

00:06:26.277 --> 00:06:28.629
as leadership and then it become joyful.

00:06:28.629 --> 00:06:30.259
Then you start experiencing happiness

00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:32.759
because it's two people in a relationship

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:36.926
caring about each other
moving an objective forward,

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.129
celebrating victory.

00:06:40.129 --> 00:06:44.197
Celebration becomes the
currency for momentum

00:06:44.197 --> 00:06:47.227
and this is happiness, this is happiness.

00:06:47.227 --> 00:06:48.568
Then you're getting in the groove and

00:06:48.568 --> 00:06:51.386
you're in the fly wheel of mutual support

00:06:51.386 --> 00:06:53.729
and giving a damn and
people around you that care.

00:06:53.729 --> 00:06:58.402
I mean (bleep) that's happiness.

00:06:58.402 --> 00:07:00.271
So he were were sitting
along the side of the road,

00:07:00.271 --> 00:07:02.686
it's November, Christmas is coming up.

00:07:02.686 --> 00:07:06.506
And along the side of
the road, a few yards

00:07:06.506 --> 00:07:10.180
in front of like the
car is a pile of trash

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.885
and in that pile of
trash, there was a toy.

00:07:12.885 --> 00:07:14.245
I don't know if you know
what a Big Wheel is,

00:07:14.245 --> 00:07:18.290
like a three-wheel plastic
toy thing sticking out.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:20.108
Dad sees it.

00:07:20.108 --> 00:07:21.713
He's like wait.

00:07:21.713 --> 00:07:22.774
I know what he's going to go do.

00:07:22.774 --> 00:07:24.197
He's going to go get me that big wheel.

00:07:24.197 --> 00:07:25.351
It's November.

00:07:25.351 --> 00:07:27.627
We had been unemployed
for literally six months

00:07:27.627 --> 00:07:29.763
at that time and back then
when you were unemployed

00:07:29.763 --> 00:07:32.645
for six months, you lost
all of your pension.

00:07:32.645 --> 00:07:35.754
When my pop died, we
didn't have a pension,

00:07:35.754 --> 00:07:38.314
we didn't have any savings and we had

00:07:38.314 --> 00:07:40.102
a $5,000 life insurance policy that wasn't

00:07:40.102 --> 00:07:42.269
enough to bury him, right.

00:07:43.637 --> 00:07:46.665
And, you know, we had to accept gifts

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:49.512
to be able to bury my pop.

00:07:49.512 --> 00:07:52.595
So there we were and here I am afraid

00:07:54.791 --> 00:07:56.478
that my friends from my snooty school

00:07:56.478 --> 00:07:58.225
are going to see us picking up trash.

00:07:58.225 --> 00:08:00.641
So I'm hiding in the back of the car.

00:08:00.641 --> 00:08:03.653
What do you think it takes for a proud

00:08:03.653 --> 00:08:06.335
55-year-old steelworker to walk up and

00:08:06.335 --> 00:08:09.195
get a piece of trash with
his son still in the car,

00:08:09.195 --> 00:08:12.112
visibly embarrassed, piece of trash

00:08:13.403 --> 00:08:14.624
for his son's Christmas present

00:08:14.624 --> 00:08:16.673
because that's all he could do.

00:08:16.673 --> 00:08:17.689
You know what?

00:08:17.689 --> 00:08:18.702
My dad didn't care.

00:08:18.702 --> 00:08:21.329
My dad wanted to make
sure that he had something

00:08:21.329 --> 00:08:24.231
for his family and that's what mattered.

00:08:24.231 --> 00:08:25.532
And he always said the same thing to me,

00:08:25.532 --> 00:08:27.663
Keith, don't ever be afraid to ask.

00:08:27.663 --> 00:08:28.846
The worst anybody could ever say was no.

00:08:28.846 --> 00:08:32.071
The first of two very important lessons

00:08:32.071 --> 00:08:34.777
in this story to all of us.

00:08:34.777 --> 00:08:36.219
How many times have you backed down

00:08:36.219 --> 00:08:39.208
from what you knew you needed to do

00:08:39.208 --> 00:08:41.791
because you were afraid to ask.

00:08:42.883 --> 00:08:45.115
I really hope you send
this message to your kids

00:08:45.115 --> 00:08:47.051
and grandkids because
there are so many times

00:08:47.051 --> 00:08:49.692
that I didn't think I
deserved to be in that room,

00:08:49.692 --> 00:08:51.944
so many times that I didn't
think that those rich people

00:08:51.944 --> 00:08:54.037
would accept me, that
I just plowed through,

00:08:54.037 --> 00:08:56.641
pushed through because
my dad would always,

00:08:56.641 --> 00:08:57.999
his voice would always come up.

00:08:57.999 --> 00:08:59.052
Don't ever be afraid to ask.

00:08:59.052 --> 00:09:01.070
The worst anybody could ever say is no.

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:02.446
- The challenge that
is see with most people

00:09:02.446 --> 00:09:04.239
is they don't have a support group,

00:09:04.239 --> 00:09:06.144
no accountability group or a master mind

00:09:06.144 --> 00:09:07.328
or whatever you want to call it.

00:09:07.328 --> 00:09:10.144
So how does someone want first find that

00:09:10.144 --> 00:09:11.636
and figure out who the right people are

00:09:11.636 --> 00:09:13.057
that aren't going to suck
the energy from them,

00:09:13.057 --> 00:09:14.642
but also are going to give
and take at the same time.

00:09:14.642 --> 00:09:15.635
- It's trial and error.

00:09:15.635 --> 00:09:16.633
- Yeah.

00:09:16.633 --> 00:09:17.648
- When I wrote Who's Got Your Back,

00:09:17.648 --> 00:09:19.071
there's a chapter in it that talks

00:09:19.071 --> 00:09:20.224
about the long slow dinner.

00:09:20.224 --> 00:09:23.252
I think by the time we're done here

00:09:23.252 --> 00:09:25.102
in a short period of
time, I want everybody to

00:09:25.102 --> 00:09:28.580
watching us to have a
relationship action plan.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:32.232
So, as we're sitting here
scribble down your goals

00:09:32.232 --> 00:09:36.097
and next to every goal,
start writing three to five

00:09:36.097 --> 00:09:38.535
people's names critical
to achieving those goals.

00:09:38.535 --> 00:09:39.546
- Interesting.

00:09:39.546 --> 00:09:40.730
- That's the opportunity group.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:45.337
Then I want you to ask
yourself who to achieve my goal

00:09:45.337 --> 00:09:47.272
do I want to learn from.

00:09:47.272 --> 00:09:48.666
Write those names down.

00:09:48.666 --> 00:09:50.902
Then start imagining who are the people

00:09:50.902 --> 00:09:53.731
that I could trust around four core,

00:09:53.731 --> 00:09:55.126
there are four core characteristics

00:09:55.126 --> 00:09:57.552
of the lifeline relationship and frankly,

00:09:57.552 --> 00:09:59.461
all relationships, but the tightest ones

00:09:59.461 --> 00:10:00.763
have to have four things.

00:10:00.763 --> 00:10:04.176
Number one is intimacy to
the point of vulnerability.

00:10:04.176 --> 00:10:08.051
Can you I tell you when
I'm really feeling weak,

00:10:08.051 --> 00:10:09.580
can I tell you when I'm
up against the wall.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:11.287
Intimacy to the point of vulnerability.

00:10:11.287 --> 00:10:13.232
Second is generosity.

00:10:13.232 --> 00:10:15.368
Do I really want to help you and do you

00:10:15.368 --> 00:10:16.581
really want to help me.

00:10:16.581 --> 00:10:19.549
Do we care enough to help, generosity.

00:10:19.549 --> 00:10:21.214
Candor, will you tell me the truth.

00:10:21.214 --> 00:10:22.218
Right?

00:10:22.218 --> 00:10:24.628
Most intimate critical relationships

00:10:24.628 --> 00:10:27.797
lie to each other and they shouldn't.

00:10:27.797 --> 00:10:30.606
Candor and conflict avoidance is horrible.

00:10:30.606 --> 00:10:31.604
- Why to lie to each other,

00:10:31.604 --> 00:10:32.597
because they just don't want to make each

00:10:32.597 --> 00:10:33.611
other feel bad?

00:10:33.611 --> 00:10:35.073
- Plication, they think
that that's their role,

00:10:35.073 --> 00:10:36.214
to make each other feel good.

00:10:36.214 --> 00:10:38.526
Look at me, I think a lot of it is

00:10:38.526 --> 00:10:41.235
the people don't realize
that relationships

00:10:41.235 --> 00:10:42.792
need to be leaned on,

00:10:42.792 --> 00:10:44.283
the relationships aren't scarce,

00:10:44.283 --> 00:10:47.086
that there, a lot of people
are conflict avoidant

00:10:47.086 --> 00:10:48.980
out of a psychological
fear of abandonment.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:51.419
I mean, there's a lot
of insecurities and fear

00:10:51.419 --> 00:10:53.971
that drive our relational behaviors

00:10:53.971 --> 00:10:57.388
and I have to say that conflict avoidance

00:10:59.335 --> 00:11:02.586
has to be one of the most
erosive, erosive elements

00:11:02.586 --> 00:11:05.047
of organization and human society.

00:11:05.047 --> 00:11:08.380
So, the fourth is accountability, right?

00:11:10.002 --> 00:11:13.291
But candor and
accountability together make

00:11:13.291 --> 00:11:14.446
a high performing relationship.

00:11:14.446 --> 00:11:16.442
What I would do is I
would list three people

00:11:16.442 --> 00:11:19.432
that you have a strong
enough relationship with now.

00:11:19.432 --> 00:11:22.088
You and I have, frankly
you and I have a perfectly

00:11:22.088 --> 00:11:24.668
reasonable and strong enough relationship.

00:11:24.668 --> 00:11:26.218
I call it a level two relationship.

00:11:26.218 --> 00:11:28.326
Maybe we're bordering on a three,

00:11:28.326 --> 00:11:30.237
but certainly two to three and three

00:11:30.237 --> 00:11:31.417
is a strong relationship.

00:11:31.417 --> 00:11:34.200
If you want to go to a lifeline,

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.659
which is in my world a three plus,

00:11:36.659 --> 00:11:39.482
what you're going to do
is you're going to go out

00:11:39.482 --> 00:11:40.722
and have a long slow dinner.

00:11:40.722 --> 00:11:43.054
You're going to take your armor off

00:11:43.054 --> 00:11:46.373
and you're going to say, look,
here's what I want to do,

00:11:46.373 --> 00:11:49.949
here's what I'm afraid of and here's how I

00:11:49.949 --> 00:11:51.814
think I'm going to get
there, what do you think.

00:11:51.814 --> 00:11:53.623
And see how the person responds.

00:11:53.623 --> 00:11:55.083
Do they respond with candor?

00:11:55.083 --> 00:11:57.382
Do they respond with intimacy?

00:11:57.382 --> 00:11:59.190
Do they let their guards down?

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:01.663
Do they respond with accountability?

00:12:01.663 --> 00:12:03.468
At the end of the dinner,
are they saying to you,

00:12:03.468 --> 00:12:06.241
dude, let's talk next week because I think

00:12:06.241 --> 00:12:07.841
you should do these two or three things

00:12:07.841 --> 00:12:10.505
and I'm going to hold you
accountable for them damn it.

00:12:10.505 --> 00:12:12.392
Now if after the dinner,
they don't call you

00:12:12.392 --> 00:12:13.959
the following week, then maybe they're not

00:12:13.959 --> 00:12:17.169
the right person to be
your lifeline, right?

00:12:17.169 --> 00:12:19.124
I know that was a very circuitous way

00:12:19.124 --> 00:12:20.361
to answer your question.

00:12:20.361 --> 00:12:21.359
- [Interview] That's great.

00:12:21.359 --> 00:12:22.373
- What do most people do wrong

00:12:22.373 --> 00:12:23.975
when they approach
relationships and networks

00:12:23.975 --> 00:12:25.318
in your experience?

00:12:25.318 --> 00:12:27.940
- It's a mindset and it's a mindset

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:30.499
of what's in it for me, right?

00:12:30.499 --> 00:12:31.599
That's the first thing.

00:12:31.599 --> 00:12:33.428
It's a mindset of what's in it for me

00:12:33.428 --> 00:12:34.461
and it stinks on their suit a mile away.

00:12:34.461 --> 00:12:35.473
What's in it for me?

00:12:35.473 --> 00:12:38.640
It needs to be a values based judgment

00:12:39.481 --> 00:12:40.783
of how you're going to live your life

00:12:40.783 --> 00:12:42.530
and you've got to, now by the way,

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:46.025
there's an AA slogan called
fake it till you make it.

00:12:46.025 --> 00:12:48.232
So, you know, the point is that little

00:12:48.232 --> 00:12:50.959
by little by little, you've got to be able

00:12:50.959 --> 00:12:53.042
to recondition your brain

00:12:54.043 --> 00:12:55.574
and that's one of the things about

00:12:55.574 --> 00:12:56.590
human behavior change.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.325
Human behavior change doesn't
happen through knowledge.

00:12:59.325 --> 00:13:01.035
If it happened through knowledge,

00:13:01.035 --> 00:13:03.660
then nobody on a diet would
eat a piece of cake, right?

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:08.293
I mean, the bottom line
is we need to experience

00:13:08.293 --> 00:13:11.155
behavior change, small
doses of incremental

00:13:11.155 --> 00:13:13.222
experiences, positive experiences,

00:13:13.222 --> 00:13:14.347
not even negative experiences.

00:13:14.347 --> 00:13:17.164
Small doses of incremental
positive experiences

00:13:17.164 --> 00:13:20.175
change mindsets, so if
you change the behavior,

00:13:20.175 --> 00:13:21.174
you change the mindset.

00:13:21.174 --> 00:13:23.841
We unfortunately have addictions

00:13:24.709 --> 00:13:26.997
and I use the word addiction and I follow

00:13:26.997 --> 00:13:28.976
things like 12-step programs
and other mechanisms

00:13:28.976 --> 00:13:31.694
to shift behavioral addictions

00:13:31.694 --> 00:13:33.686
because everybody who's watching this

00:13:33.686 --> 00:13:35.251
has an addiction.

00:13:35.251 --> 00:13:37.382
There's not a single
person that isn't addicted

00:13:37.382 --> 00:13:41.016
to feeling less than in
a room, procrastination.

00:13:41.016 --> 00:13:45.654
You know, some behavioral
addiction that holds us back,

00:13:45.654 --> 00:13:48.214
you know, being too
shy, being to insecure,

00:13:48.214 --> 00:13:50.164
being too whatever, tempers.

00:13:50.164 --> 00:13:51.789
All these things are nothing more than

00:13:51.789 --> 00:13:53.435
addictive behaviors that we practice even

00:13:53.435 --> 00:13:55.203
though they don't serve us well.

00:13:55.203 --> 00:13:57.664
So little tiny doses of new behaviors,

00:13:57.664 --> 00:14:00.466
pushing yourself incrementally will be

00:14:00.466 --> 00:14:02.862
the way in which we
change those behaviors.

00:14:02.862 --> 00:14:05.727
